  "preview": "Mira starts the drone racing league with $52. Every weekend she earns prize money and pays for parts. When the season ends, the bills keep coming but the prize money stops. How long until she's broke?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Model a two-phase savings situation with positive and negative rates of change. Combine a starting amount with net earnings over a fixed window, then solve a one-variable equation to find when a balance reaches zero.",
    "studentPrompt": "Mira competes in a regional drone racing league. She begins the summer with $52 saved up.\n\nThe racing season runs for 8 weeks. Each week she:\n- earns $95 in prize money and sponsor stipends, and\n- spends $28 on replacement props, batteries, and repairs.\n\nWhen the season ends, the prize money stops, but she still has to pay $54 per week in drone club dues and storage. She wants to know how long her savings will last before she has to find a new way to earn money.\n\n1. How much money will Mira have in her account the day the racing season ends?\n2. Starting from that amount, how many full weeks of off-season can she pay for before her balance drops below zero?\n3. On exactly what day (counting from the end of the season) does her balance hit $0?\n4. Mira's coach offers her a deal: skip the last 2 weeks of racing to coach beginners for $40/week (no parts cost). Would taking the deal make her off-season savings last longer or shorter? Justify with numbers.",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A small quadcopter drone hovers over a stylized race-gate course at sunset, with a checkered flag in the foreground. The scene is decorative and gives no numeric information about earnings or expenses.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"sky3\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#f5c98a\"/><stop offset=\"0.6\" stop-color=\"#e89b6a\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#7a5a8a\"/></linearGradient></defs><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"175\" fill=\"url(#sky3)\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"175\" width=\"480\" height=\"45\" fill=\"#2c3e50\"/><circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"110\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#fff0c2\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><g stroke=\"#1f2a44\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"><rect x=\"60\" y=\"95\" width=\"55\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"205\" y=\"105\" width=\"45\" height=\"70\" rx=\"4\"/></g><g stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.85\"><rect x=\"60\" y=\"95\" width=\"55\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"205\" y=\"105\" width=\"45\" height=\"70\" rx=\"4\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(150,75)\"><line x1=\"-22\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"22\" y2=\"0\" stroke=\"#1f2a44\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-14\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#1f2a44\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"-22\" cy=\"0\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" opacity=\"0.6\"/><circle cx=\"22\" cy=\"0\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" opacity=\"0.6\"/><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-14\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" opacity=\"0.6\"/><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"14\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" opacity=\"0.6\"/><rect x=\"-6\" y=\"-4\" width=\"12\" height=\"8\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#34495e\"/><circle cx=\"4\" cy=\"-2\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#e74c3c\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(400,135)\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#1f2a44\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M0,0 L28,0 L28,22 L0,22 Z\" fill=\"#f7fafc\"/><path d=\"M0,0 h7 v7 h-7 z M14,0 h7 v7 h-7 z M7,7 h7 v7 h-7 z M21,7 h7 v7 h-7 z M0,14 h7 v7 h-7 z M14,14 h7 v7 h-7 z\" fill=\"#1f2a44\"/></g><g fill=\"#fff0c2\" opacity=\"0.7\"><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"40\" r=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"30\" r=\"1\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"50\" r=\"1.3\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"35\" r=\"1\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"60\" r=\"1.2\"/></g><path d=\"M0,180 Q120,178 240,182 T480,180\" stroke=\"#1f2a44\" stroke-width=\"1\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.5\"/></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show the image. Ask kids if anyone has ever tried to budget for a hobby — paintball, gymnastics meets, gaming PC parts, anything. Pose the situation: 'Mira is running a small operation. Money is coming in, money is going out, and then one day the money stops coming in. Let's figure out how long she can keep flying.' Send to vertical boards.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: calculators (let groups decide whether to use them)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "rates-of-change",
      "signed-numbers",
      "linear-equations",
      "two-phase-modeling",
      "real-world-application"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. Q1 is computational (low floor — every group can get something). Q2 invites either repeated subtraction or division, which becomes a great discussion. Q3 forces a precise interpretation of 'when balance hits zero' — fractional weeks, days, rounding direction. Q4 is the rich extension that rewards modeling, not just calculation.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Net weekly earnings = $95 - $28 = $67. Over 8 weeks: 8 x $67 = $536. Plus starting $52 = $588 at season's end. Some groups will add row by row in a table; others jump to a single multiplication.\n- Q2: With $588 saved and $54/week going out, $588 / $54 ≈ 10.89 weeks. So 10 full weeks; on week 11 she runs out partway through. Expect arguments about whether the answer is 10 or 11.\n- Q3: 588 - 54w = 0 → w = 588/54 = 10.888… weeks. That's 10 weeks and 0.888 x 7 ≈ 6.2 days. So roughly day 76 from season's end (week 11, day 6).\n- Q4: Skipping 2 weeks of racing loses 2 x $67 = $134 in net earnings. Coaching adds 2 x $40 = $80. Net change at season's end = -$54. New balance = $534. Off-season: $534 / $54 ≈ 9.89 weeks. She loses ~1 week of off-season. Deal is worse.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating $95 as Mira's weekly profit and forgetting to subtract the $28 in parts.\n- Computing 8 x $95 = $760 and forgetting to subtract 8 x $28.\n- For Q2, dividing $588 by $54 and reporting '10.89 weeks' as a final answer without interpreting what the decimal means in days.\n- For Q4, comparing $40 to $95 instead of $40 to the net $67.\n- For Q4, forgetting that the parts cost ($28) also disappears when she skips racing.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two groups got $588 for Q1, but one used a table and one used 8(95-28)+52. Which method made the structure clearer? When would the table be worth the extra time?\n- For Q2, what's the difference between '10 full weeks' and '10.89 weeks'? Which one answers the question?\n- For Q4, the coaching deal pays real money. Why does it still leave Mira worse off?\n- If Mira's starting balance had been $0 instead of $52, would any of your answers change? Which ones, by how much?",
    "extensions": "- Mira's older brother offers to match every dollar she saves during the racing season, up to a maximum of $200. How does that change her off-season runway?\n- Write a linear function B(w) for her balance w weeks after the season ends. Graph it. What does the slope mean? The y-intercept? The x-intercept?\n- Mira's club dues drop to $30/week if she signs a 6-month contract up front for $150. Should she take the contract? At what off-season length does the contract become the better deal?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Net weekly earnings during the season = 95 - 28 = $67. Over 8 weeks she nets 8 x 67 = $536. Plus the starting $52, her end-of-season balance is $52 + $536 = $588.\n2. Off-season she loses $54/week. 588 / 54 = 10.888…, so she can pay for 10 full weeks of off-season. Week 11 she runs out mid-week.\n3. Solve 588 - 54w = 0 → w = 588/54 ≈ 10.89 weeks ≈ 10 weeks + 0.89(7) ≈ 10 weeks, 6 days. So roughly 76 days after the season ends.\n4. With the coaching deal: she loses 2(67) = $134 from skipping racing but gains 2(40) = $80 coaching, a net change of -$54. New end-of-season balance = 588 - 54 = $534. Off-season runway: 534/54 ≈ 9.89 weeks. She loses about 1 week. The deal is worse for her finances.",
